'''Kaina Shrine''' ({{lang|ja|甲斐奈神社}}, ''Kaina Jinja'') refers to a group of {{ill|Shinto shrine|en}} with the name "Kaina" located in {{ill|Yamanashi Prefecture|ja|山梨県|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, Japan. There are three such shrines, all of which are {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft}} listed in the {{ill|Engishiki|ja|延喜式神名帳|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|qq=draft|qq=draft}} under "Kaina Shrine, Yamanashi District, Kai Province." 

== Overview ==
The three Kaina Shrines are {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft}} contenders ({{ill|Ronsha|ja|論社|qq=draft|qq=draft}}), meaning their historical authenticity as the original shrine listed in the ''Engishiki'' is debated. In total, there are four contenders for the title:

1. '''{{ill|Kaina Shrine (Fuefuki, Kasugai)|ja|甲斐奈神社 (笛吹市春日居町国府)|qq=draft|qq=draft}}''' (Kasugai, Fuefuki City) - Associated with {{ill|Kai Province|ja|甲斐国|qq=draft|qq=draft}}'s {{ill|Sōja|ja|総社|qq=draft|qq=draft}} and considered the "Fourth Shrine" of the province. Former {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.
2. '''{{ill|Kaina Shrine (Fuefuki, Hashitate)|ja|甲斐奈神社 (笛吹市一宮町橋立)|qq=draft|qq=draft}}''' (Hashitate, Fuefuki City) - Another Sōja contender, formerly a {{ill|Mura-sha|ja|村社|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.
3. '''{{ill|Kaina Shrine (Kofu)|ja|甲斐奈神社 (甲府市)|qq=draft|qq=draft}}''' (Kofu City) - Former Gōsha.
4. '''Azumaya-no-Miya''' (Chinme, Fuefuki City) - A {{ill|Sessha|ja|摂社|qq=draft|qq=draft}} of {{ill|Yamanashioka Shrine|ja|山梨岡神社|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.

The shifting locations of the {{ill|Kai Province Government Office|ja|甲斐国府|qq=draft|qq=draft}}—from Kasugai to the vicinity of Hashitate, and eventually to Kofu—may explain the presence of multiple contenders for the Sōja title. The name "Kaina" could derive from "Kai no" ("of Kai Province") or other interpretations such as "Kai no Na" ("Kai's greens").<ref name="chosa">{{Cite book|title=Shikinaisha Survey Report|publisher=皇學館大学出版部|year=1985}}</ref><ref>Alternative interpretations suggest "Kaina" could mean "Kai's vegetables." (''Shikinaisha Survey Report'')</ref>

In the {{ill|Sengoku period (Japan)|ja|戦国時代|qq=draft|qq=draft}}, the {{ill|Takeda clan|ja|武田氏|qq=draft|qq=draft}} established the {{ill|Fuchū Hachiman Shrine|ja|府中八幡宮|qq=draft|qq=draft}} in Kofu, which took over the role of the province's Sōja.

Debates over the Ronsha have persisted since the publication of the ''{{ill|Kai Koku-shi|ja|甲斐国志|qq=draft|qq=draft}}.'' Modern scholars, including those in the ''Shikinaisha Survey Report'' by Kogakkan University, remain undecided on the matter.<ref name="chosa"/>

