'''Kaina Shrine''' ({{lang|ja|甲斐奈神社}}, ''Kaina Jinja'') refers to a group of {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinto shrine|qq=}} with the name "Kaina" located in {{ill|Yamanashi Prefecture|ja|山梨県|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamanashi Prefecture|qq=}}, Japan. There are three such shrines, all of which are {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|qq=}} listed in the {{ill|Engishiki|ja|延喜式神名帳|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Engishiki|13=User:Immanuelle/Engishiki Jinmyōchō|qq=}} under "Kaina Shrine, Yamanashi District, Kai Province." 

== Overview ==
The three Kaina Shrines are {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|qq=}} contenders ({{ill|Ronsha|ja|論社|1=candidate shrines|2=ja|3=論社|lt=candidate|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ronsha|13=User:Immanuelle/candidate shrines|qq=}}), meaning their historical authenticity as the original shrine listed in the ''Engishiki'' is debated. In total, there are four contenders for the title:

1. '''{{ill|Kaina Shrine (Fuefuki, Kasugai)|ja|甲斐奈神社 (笛吹市春日居町国府)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kaina Shrine (Fuefuki, Kasugai)|qq=}}''' (Kasugai, Fuefuki City) - Associated with {{ill|Kai Province|ja|甲斐国|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kai Province|qq=}}'s {{ill|Sōja|ja|総社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sōja|qq=}} and considered the "Fourth Shrine" of the province. Former {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gōsha|qq=}}.
2. '''{{ill|Kaina Shrine (Fuefuki, Hashitate)|ja|甲斐奈神社 (笛吹市一宮町橋立)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kaina Shrine (Fuefuki, Hashitate)|qq=}}''' (Hashitate, Fuefuki City) - Another Sōja contender, formerly a {{ill|Mura-sha|ja|村社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mura-sha|qq=}}.
3. '''{{ill|Kaina Shrine (Kofu)|ja|甲斐奈神社 (甲府市)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kaina Shrine (Kofu)|qq=}}''' (Kofu City) - Former Gō-sha.
4. '''Azumaya-no-Miya''' (Chinme, Fuefuki City) - A {{ill|Sessha|ja|摂社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sessha|qq=}} of {{ill|Yamanashioka Shrine|ja|山梨岡神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yamanashioka Shrine|qq=}}.

The shifting locations of the {{ill|Kai Province Government Office|ja|甲斐国府|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kai Province Government Office|qq=}}—from Kasugai to the vicinity of Hashitate, and eventually to Kofu—may explain the presence of multiple contenders for the Sōja title. The name "Kaina" could derive from "Kai no" ("of Kai Province") or other interpretations such as "Kai no Na" ("Kai's greens").<ref name="chosa">{{Cite book|title=Shikinaisha Survey Report|publisher=皇學館大学出版部|year=1985}}</ref><ref>Alternative interpretations suggest "Kaina" could mean "Kai's vegetables." (''Shikinaisha Survey Report'')</ref>

In the {{ill|Sengoku period (Japan)|ja|戦国時代|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sengoku period (Japan)|qq=}}, the {{ill|Takeda clan|ja|武田氏|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takeda clan|qq=}} established the {{ill|Fuchū Hachiman Shrine|ja|府中八幡宮|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fuchū Hachiman Shrine|qq=}} in Kofu, which took over the role of the province's Sōja.

Debates over the Ronsha have persisted since the publication of the ''{{ill|Kai Koku-shi|ja|甲斐国志|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kai Koku-shi|qq=}}.'' Modern scholars, including those in the ''Shikinaisha Survey Report'' by Kogakkan University, remain undecided on the matter.<ref name="chosa"/>

